---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const { summary, statusDisagreement, vessels } = data;
const n = (v: number, d = 0) =>
  v.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });

const share = (statusDisagreement.disagreeing / statusDisagreement.totalStops) * 100;
const both = vessels.filter((v) => v.bothDirections).slice(0, 5);
const eta = data.rules.find((r) => r.ruleId === 'R13');
---
<Base
  title="Who forgot the dial? — AIS navigational status, checked against the ships' own movement"
  description="Across seven days of Danish AIS, 37% of stopped ships still broadcast that they were under way. An open pipeline that checks a vessel's self-report against its own measured movement."
>
  <h1>A third of stopped ships still say they're under way</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    Every AIS message carries two things: a position the receiver measured, and a navigational
    status a human typed in. This is what happens when you check one against the other.
  </p>

  <ul class="stats">
    <li class="stat alarm"><b>{n(share, 0)}%</b><span>of stops claimed "under way"</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(summary.hoursClaimingUnderWay)}</b><span>hours parked, claiming to steam</span></li>
    <li class="stat alarm"><b>{n(summary.departureLapses)}</b><span>fixes claiming moored, while moving</span></li>
    <li class="stat alarm"><b>{n(eta?.flagged ?? 0)}</b><span>messages with an ETA over a year out</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The dial is set once, or never</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    The interesting part isn't that crews forget. It's that they mostly don't forget <em>sometimes</em>.
    Of the vessels that stopped at least {3} times in the window, <strong>{summary.vesselsAlwaysWrong}</strong>
    got it wrong on <em>every single stop</em>, and <strong>{summary.vesselsAlwaysRight}</strong> got it
    right on every single one. Far fewer sit in between than chance would put there.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="prose">
    <p>
      That looks less like a crew occasionally forgetting and more like a setting configured once at
      installation and never touched again — by some ships, and not by others.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Two ways to get it wrong</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong><a href="/arrivals">Forgot on arrival</a></strong> — the ship stops, and the status
      stays on "under way using engine". Detected on {n(statusDisagreement.disagreeing)} of
      {n(statusDisagreement.totalStops)} stops. These vessels moved a mean of 92 metres over a mean
      of 5.7 hours; a ship genuinely under way at one knot covers ten kilometres in that time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong><a href="/departures">Forgot on departure</a></strong> — the ship leaves, and the
      status stays on "moored" or "at anchor". Detected on {n(summary.departureLapses)} position
      reports. The fastest was doing 15.5 knots while telling the world it was tied up.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A third field, going stale sideways</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    The ETA is hand-typed too, and it rots the same way — but it shows up <em>inverted</em>, because
    of a quirk in the encoding. <strong>The AIS ETA field has no year.</strong> It carries month,
    day, hour and minute and nothing else, so a decoder meeting a date that has already gone by has
    to assume the next one. A forgotten ETA is never left behind in the past; it gets rolled forward
    and reappears up to a year ahead.
  </p>

  <p class="prose">
    You can see it in the data as a hole. Across {n(4800000)} messages carrying an ETA, the count
    falls away smoothly to about a month out — and then there is <strong>not one single message</strong>
    anywhere between 42 and 76 days ahead, before the numbers climb again and pile up at almost
    exactly one year. Two populations with nothing in between: real voyage plans, and dates nobody
    has touched since they passed. {n(eta?.flagged ?? 0)} messages sit in the far one.
  </p>

  <h2>Forgot in both directions</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    {both.length} vessels here managed both — leaving the dial alone on the way in and on the way out.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>vessel</th><th class="n">stops wrong</th><th class="n">fixes wrong</th><th class="n">fastest kn</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {both.map((v) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={`/vessel/${v.mmsi}`}>{v.name ?? '(no name broadcast)'}</a></td>
            <td class="n">{n(v.arrivalLapses)} of {n(v.totalStops)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(v.departureLapses)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(v.fastestKnWhileClaimingStationary, 1)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Before you blame anyone</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Three things could produce these numbers: a crew that never touched the setting, an
      installation that was configured once and left, or a decoding fault somewhere between the
      transponder and this page. The evidence points at the first two — a decode fault would scatter
      randomly, and this doesn't scatter — but the pipeline reports what was <em>broadcast</em>, and
      never who was at fault.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's a further wrinkle worth enjoying: the vessel names on this site are
      <a href="/how-ais-works">also typed in by hand</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
